===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A [[measure]] of cold or heat, often measurable with a [[thermometer]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:temperature}}
#: {{ux|en|The boiling '''temperature''' of pure water is 100 degrees [[Celsius]].}}
#: {{ux|en|The '''temperature''' in the room dropped nearly 20 degrees; it went from hot to cold.}}
#: {{ux|en|The most accurate way to take your '''temperature''' is by sticking a thermometer up your butt.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-05-11|volume=407|issue=8835|page=80|magazine=w:The Economist
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/science-and-technology/21577341-worlds-third-largest-area-ice-about-undergo-systematic The climate of Tibet: Pole-land]
|passage=Of all the transitions brought about on the Earth’s surface by '''temperature''' change, the melting of ice into water is the starkest. It is binary. And for the land beneath, the air above and the life around, it changes everything.}}
# An elevated body temperature, as present in [[fever]] and many [[illness]]es.
#: {{ux|en|You have a '''temperature'''. I think you should stay home today. You’re sick.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1951|author=Josephine Tey|title=The Daughter of Time|text="Aren't you feeling so well this morning?" she asked him anxiously. "Do you think you've got a '''temperature'''?"}}
# {{lb|en|thermodynamics}} A property of macroscopic amounts of matter that serves to gauge the average intensity of the random ''actual'' motions of the individually mobile particulate constituents.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Clinton D. Stoner|title=Inquiries into the Nature of Free Energy and Entropy in Respect to Biochemical Thermodynamics|work=Entropy|page=106–141|year=2000|month=September|url=https://www.mdpi.com/1099-4300/2/3/106|volume=2|issue=3|issn=1099-4300|doi=10.3390/e2030106|passage=In consequence, macroscopic amounts of matter in thermal contact with one another tend to be at the same '''temperature''', a fact of sufficient fundamental importance to merit belated designation as the Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics.}}
# {{lb|en|machine learning}} A [[parameter]] that controls the degree of [[randomness]] of the [[output]].
# {{lb|en|figurative|colloquial}} The general [[mood]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Seth Schiesel|title=Taking the Temperature of the Creative Body|work=The New York Times|date=2005-08-20|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2005/08/20/sports/othersports/taking-the-temperature-of-the-creative-body.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=But it is both easier and more accurate to take the industry's true '''temperature''' at small private gatherings like a conference organized by the Ziff Davis publishing company in northern California last week.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The state or condition of being [[tempered]] or moderated.
# {{lb|en|now|_|rare|archaic}} The balance of [[humours]] in the body, or one's character or outlook as considered determined from this; [[temperament]].
#* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy|edition=2nd|passage=Our intemperence it is that pulls so many several incurable diseases on our heads, that hastens old age, perverts our '''temperature''', and brings upon us sudden death.}}
#* {{RQ:Sterne Tristram Shandy|passage={{...}}that not only the production of a rational Being was concern'd in it, but that possibly the happy foundation and '''temperature''' of his body, perhaps his genius and the very cast of his mind {{...}}}}
#* '''1993''', James Michie, trans. Ovid, ''The Art of Love'', Book II:
#*: Only a strong dose of love will cure / A woman with an angry '''temperature'''.

====Quotations====
* {{quote-text|en|year=2007|author=James Shipman; Jerry Wilson; Aaron Todd|title=An Introduction to Physical Science: Twelfth Edition|pages=106–108
|passage=Heat and '''temperature''', although different, are intimately related. {{...}} For example, suppose you added equal amounts of heat to equal masses of iron and aluminum. How do you think their '''temperatures''' would change? {{...}} if the '''temperature''' of the iron increased by 100 C&deg;, the corresponding '''temperature''' change in the aluminum would be only 48 C&deg;.}}

